# Networking / API client

**Primary source:** OkHttp — the de facto universal HTTP engine
underneath both Retrofit and Ktor's Android engine. Neither Retrofit nor
Ktor is a Jetpack/Google-first-party library; this is the one layer where
Android has no first-party option, same as iOS.

**Community layer:** Retrofit + OkHttp (Interceptor + Authenticator) as
the production-composition layer — matches Google's own "Now in Android"
sample app's networking stack. Do not hand-roll an equivalent wrapper
around raw OkHttp. Ktor is an ADR-gated alternative
(`android-adr.mdc` trigger #3) for Kotlin Multiplatform requirements.

## One client, not scattered calls

Every network call in the app goes through a single `ApiClient`
Retrofit interface, injected via Hilt (`dependency-injection-hilt.mdc`).
No feature constructs its own `OkHttpClient`/`Retrofit` instance or calls
`okHttpClient.newCall(...)` directly.

```kotlin
interface ApiClient {
    @GET("system-status")
    suspend fun getSystemStatus(): SystemStatusResponse
}
```

## Live composition

```kotlin
val okHttpClient = OkHttpClient.Builder()
    .addInterceptor(AuthInterceptor(tokenStore))
    .addInterceptor(CorrelationIdInterceptor())
    .addInterceptor(LoggingInterceptor(logger))
    .authenticator(TokenAuthenticator(authRepository))
    .build()
```

- **`AuthInterceptor`** injects `Authorization: Bearer <token>` from the
  current session token (`security-baseline-mobile.mdc` for
  DataStore/Keystore-backed storage).
- **`CorrelationIdInterceptor`** injects an `X-Correlation-Id` header,
  sourced from the same correlation id used by logging
  (`logging-observability.mdc`) — so a single id ties a user action to
  both the client-side log and the request the backend receives.
- **`LoggingInterceptor`** logs method, path, status, and duration around
  every request through the app's `AppLogger` — never `Log.d`/`println`
  directly.
- **`TokenAuthenticator`** (OkHttp's `Authenticator`, not a generic
  `Interceptor` — this is the API purpose-built for 401 handling)
  refreshes the token exactly once per concurrent burst of 401s (guard
  with a `Mutex`, never a blocking lock, per `coroutines-flow.mdc`), then
  retries the original request. On refresh failure, it triggers
  `AuthRepository`'s logout path — call sites never handle 401
  individually.

## Typed errors

```kotlin
sealed class ApiError : Exception() {
    data object Unauthorized : ApiError()
    data class Network(val cause: Throwable) : ApiError()
    data class Decoding(val cause: Throwable) : ApiError()
    data class Server(val statusCode: Int, val body: String?) : ApiError()
}
```

- The `ApiClient` wrapper (a thin `Repository`-style class, not the raw
  Retrofit interface itself) maps every OkHttp/Retrofit failure into
  `ApiError` — `ViewModel`s and other services never inspect raw
  `HttpException`/`IOException` cases.

## Endpoint definition

- One Retrofit interface per upstream service/feature, grouped the same
  way `project-structure-gradle.mdc` groups `core`/`feature` modules.
- Request/response DTOs are pure data classes with a serialization
  annotation (Moshi or kotlinx.serialization, pick one per ADR and stay
  consistent) — no networking logic on the DTO itself.

## Testing

- `MockApiClient`/a fake `Repository` implementation (in the test source
  set) returns a fixed `Result`/sealed value per test — no real network
  calls in unit tests.
- `TokenAuthenticator`'s single-flight behavior gets its own unit test
  simulating concurrent 401s to prove only one refresh call fires.

## Anti-patterns

- **A second `OkHttpClient`/`Retrofit` instance constructed inside a
  feature** instead of the shared Hilt-provided one.
- **Handling 401 locally in a `ViewModel`** instead of relying on
  `TokenAuthenticator` + `AuthRepository`'s centralized logout.
- **Business logic inside an `Interceptor`** — interceptors stay focused
  on cross-cutting networking concerns (auth, correlation id, logging),
  not domain logic.

## Related rules

- **DI:** `dependency-injection-hilt.mdc`
- **Logging/correlation:** `logging-observability.mdc`
- **Auth/token storage:** `security-baseline-mobile.mdc`
- **Coroutines (Mutex for single-flight refresh):** `coroutines-flow.mdc`
